--TEST--
A `catch` clause naming two classes is refused — the binding carries one static type (`rule:types/declaration`'s `catch` row, `rule:expressions/catch-expression`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// PHP's multi-class clause parses here because the ordinary type grammar's
// union is reused, and it is refused rather than supported: `$e` would have
// to be two things at once. The rewrite is one clause per class, each with
// its own variable name, or one clause naming a class they both extend.
try {
    throw new LogicError("either one");
} catch (LogicError | RuntimeError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0245]: a `catch` clause names one class
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
